A portrait of different military cooks from all over Europe who served in various armies and witnessed the European wars of the 20th century. Their recollections tap into a subjective view of historical events and one that diverges in some respects from conventional history.
Cooking History take us behind the scenes of dates, facts, declarations of war, battles, and peace agreements, showing the secrets one won’t find in textbooks.
The directness of the anecdotes and destinies of the people in this film conveys a sense of life and death in the war apparatus, a sense of hope, longing, and survival strategies in the midst of destruction and despair.
Evita Peron is Argentina’s most important feminine myth. Born the illegitimate daughter of a land owner, she died as the country’s first lady.
She was a self-made, indomitable woman who was drawn into the workings of power; she captivated the Argentinean people and gave women the vote.
And she accomplished all of this surprisingly catapulted by a long visit to Franco’s Spain.
Original language: Catalan and Spanish
