In the autumn of 2007, Pasqual Maragall was diagnosed with Alzheimer’s disease. He and his family embarked on a crusade to fight the disease. And as of the very beginning of that battle, this film has become an exceptional testimony.
With intelligence, sincerity and a good disposition, Maragall allows himself to be portrayed with his family and doctors, to record his personal daily struggle.
This film presents the results of two years of following an extraordinary patient who is determined to help scientists find a cure before the 26 million people currently affected by this disease multiply by 10.
Original language: Catalan, Spanish, English, Telugu, Kannada, Dutch
Inquisitive and politically committed wartime correspondent Javier Bueno was an early-20th-century Spanish journalist who recorded everything around him on his 9.5 mm camera, allowing us today to look back on a Europe between wars.
The General
During the post-war period, hundreds of thousands of people came to Barcelona, fleeing from poverty and political persecution.
Given the lack of housing on their arrival, many of them spent years living in caves and shacks.
They were workers looking to improve their quality of life, forced to live in a shantytown.
When flats were finally built, they were constructed in substandard buildings and remote areas, a phenomenon known as “vertical shantytowns”.
Modern Barcelona has chosen to forget a part of its history, a history that is now told through the mouths of those who endured it. Images shot by amateur filmmakers contrast with the official testimony, offering a glimpse of what that city of forgotten residents was really like.
Based on rare propagandistic material, film-noir-style reconstructed sequences and exclusive filmed confessions of best selling writer John Perkins, the documentary sheds a new light to the recent American history, from the Second World War to the invasion and looting of Iraq through the confession of John Perkins. Former economic hit man, Perkins confesses in front of an angry audience in Ecuador his role working for a secret network that used loans from the World Bank, payoffs, extortion and sex, military coups and political assassinations to create the American empire.
Q&A with producer Ilias Bovalis.
This is the unknown story of Joana Biarnés, the first Spanish photojournalist.
A woman so advanced at her time that was a witness and chronicler of a key period in the twentieth century, immortalizing some of history’s greatest personalities.
Inspired by Lluis Fox’s novel "La marinada sempre arriba", different witnesses talk about the evolution of the Catalan rural society from the fifties all the way to the present.
Q&A with the director VO
Buster Keaton is a cinema projectionist who dreams of becoming a detective, just like the ones on the big screen.
Due to a misunderstanding, he finds himself involved in a case of burglary that he has not committed.
This section programmed by José Carlos Suárez comprises those productions that use archival moving images and that offer an insight into some of the most remarkable historical and social facts of our History. Memories does not exclude from its selection those titles that have already been commercially released or broadcast in a Spanish television channel by virtue of the value and interest of their content.
From the original voice of the great British auteur, Terence Davies, comes Of Time and The City. It is a visual poem which draws on the first 28 years of the director’s life – his life in Liverpool until he left in 1973. “Cut it as if it were fiction” Davies says with “images which speak” and a layered sound track of popular and classical music, voices, radio clips and a powerful, poignant voiceover by the auteur. Of Time and The City is a very personal portrait of Liverpool, beyond its Beatles and its football clubs, the home of the writer’s birth, where youth and inspiration weave his own story into the recent history of the City with fascinating found footage and counterpointed sound.
The great flight
During the first years of the Franco dictatorship, Clara Pueyo Jornet, militant of PSUC, the Catalan communist party, escaped from the prison of Les Corts in Barcelona through the main door.
She was condemned to death because of her clandestine labor on the restructuring of the Catalonia’s Unified Socialist Party. In that moment in time, her trace is lost forever.
Clara only left behind a bunch of letters, which she never sent, and some photographs.
Using archive images from that time, we trace the steps of that woman, and from so many others who fought in a repressive and conservative society.
The story of Clara is also the story of the women of her time and their fight for freedom.
Being a woman was hard, but being a revolutionary woman was even harder.
Q&A with the director VO box office
