My Iranian Paradise is a documentary film essay portraying Iran’s relationship to the Western world.
One of the directors of this film was raised in Iran. Her father was a Danish engineer and her mother a Polish survivor from one of Stalin’s Gulag camps. In this film, the life of this family is reflected in the history of Iran from the 30s and onward to the Islamic Revolution, until oil began tearing the world apart.
With the eyes of ordinary people we experience how any given person’s fate can change from one day to the next, when politics and leaderships change.
Through an understanding of its past, contemporary Iran is uncovered.
The village of La Mussara was deserted in 1967. Some of the town’s natives, now residents of other communities in the Baix Camp Region, recall their memories, anecdotes about a way of life that now forms part of the past, survival, and what it was like for them to leave their home town. In a word, the stars of this film are the testimonies of an era.
Battling with the Past
Unresolved local conflicts are passed down from generation to generation, entrenched in an irrational hatred for everything that deviates from the populist codes of the time. In the words of the director, “visualising the limits of human suffering and the unforeseeable consequences of grief is the underlying basis of this film.
Seventy years after the Spanish Civil War, we still need to work together to learn how to look each other in the eye.”
