Featuring interviews, archival film clips and footage of his sculptures, the film highlights the innovative nature of Alexander Calder (1898-1976).
The film explores Calder’s work and tries to find major recurrent themes in the life and work of one of the most revolutionary and popular artists of his day whose work still remains undeniably modern.
Q&A with director François Levy-Kuentz.
Festival Memorimage Reus 2009: Today’s Films with Yesterday’s Images is a competitive international film festival targeting films that use archival footage.
Created by the Reus City Council in 2006, Memorimage is an innovative initiative that promotes the conservation and restoration of our audiovisual heritage. An event that asserts the importance of the image in the make-up of the individual and collective memory.
For this year’s celebration, Memorimage has entrusted the programmers with the selection process for each category, placing priority on quality and seeking out the most recent film productions.
The festival schedule also includes a number of additional activities for sector professionals, students and the general community, making Memorimage a strategic point of encounter to learn about and debate the use of archival moving images. the use of archival moving images.
As a young man, Jesús Monzón created the Communist Party in Navarre. As an older man, he created the business school in Mallorca; and in between, from the Vall d’Aran valley, he set in motion a guerrilla movement to combat Franco. Yet he would always remain loyal to his friends, even if they were on the enemy’s side in the Civil War. This is the fascinating story of an unknown leader and an exceptional man.
