On the 14th of October, Reus launched the 4h edition of the Memorimage Festival – films of today with images of yesterday- which returns from the 4th to the 8th of November packed with novelties.
The festival, whose aim is to show and award films that use archive material, is the only one in the world of these characteristics.
The presentation was given by the Head of the Culture Department of Reus City Council, Empar Pont, and the festival’s Artistic Director, Anna Marquès.
Memorimage is an innovative initiative, promoted by Reus City Council that wants to show the importance of salvaging and preserving audiovisual heritage, as well as the value of images in the making of the individual and collective memory.
The goal of Reus City Council has been to maintain the spirit of the original Memorimage, created by the association Cinema Rescat, while trying to promote it further and giving it renown, making it known in the rest of Catalonia and Spain, as well as getting the citizens of Reus more involved. Memorimage should reinforce the role of the city of Reus as a cultural hub and a centre for the salvaging and preserving of images, and thus placing it in the Catalan cultural calendar of autumn festivals.
For this edition, Memorimage offers a new format, more compact, divided in four sections, and for the first time, with the peculiarity that each film will be selected by a programmer and will have its director in attendance, both of which will ensure a certain quality level. In addition, the festival has a new director on board, the experienced producer and TV expert Anna Marquès.
Memorimage will present 15 films in the following sections: the Official Section, the H for History, h for history, and Memories.
For the Official Section, dedicated to Spanish premieres of international productions, five films that have gathered awards in the foremost festivals, have been selected: Cooking History (Peter Kerekes), which narrates the trials and tribulations of various military cooks; the autobiographical Of Time and the City by Terence Davis, the gripping The Judge and the General, which follows the duel between the judge Juan Guzmán and Augusto Pinochet; My Iranian Paradise, a description of a country form its citizens’ point of view, and Revue, focussing on the USSR and the mindset of that period.
The section H for History features titles that highlight prominent facts in the history of humanity, and will be made up of three films that narrate events such as the role of television during the Cold War (Disco and Atomic War), the space race between the USA, the USSR and China (Ascension), and the exiles of political violence in Argentina during the 60s and 70s (The Loss).
The section h for history, with intimate films that tell personal stories, will bring the audience closer to the confessions of a member of the secret group Economic Hit Men who decides to admit to the blackmails and crimes committed in the name of the American empire (Apology of an Economic Hit Man); to Calder the sculptor, in a film about his life and work (Calder, Sculptor of Air), and to one of the best concerts in history, the one given by Johnny Cash in the Folsom Prison in California in 1968 (Johnny Cash at Folsom Prison).
Memories, a section devoted to national productions, will bring to the audience four pieces that range thematically from the letters sent to the radio phone-in program of Elena Francis, and the daily lives of the 50s and 60s (Querida Doña Elena); life in the slums of Barcelona in the 60s, in the long version film of the 30 minutes TV3 documentary Barraques. La Ciutat Oblidada; an interpretation of the Spanish Civil War and Franco’s repression in local code in La Batalla de la Memòria; and the history of the concentration camp of Argelers (Camp d’Argelers).
The festival will present five awards, adding up to a total of €12,000.
The City of Reus award for Best Feature Length Documentary, given by the Reus City Council, awarded with €4,000, will be for the best film in the Official Section.
The Memorimage Award to the Best Production, of €3,000, will be for open to all the Spanish premieres.
The Memorial Democràtic Award will given to the film that best advocates for the recovery of the Spanish historical memory, the fight for democratic freedom, and against Francoism during the period between 1931 and 1980, awarded with €3,000 and open to all the films in the festival; whilst the Cinema Rescat Award of €1,000 will be for the best researcher.
Furthermore, there will be an Audience Award of €1,000 given to the best film by popular vote.
The jury will be chaired by the Director of the Filmoteca of Catalonia, Roc Villas, and will be made up of four other members: Sue Malden, president of FOCAL, Tue Steen Müller, documentary expert and consultant, Lurdes Cortés, filmmaker for TVE, and Ignasi Sardà (jury for the Memorial Democràtic award only), Head of communication and promotion within the General Direction of Memorial Democràtic.
Activities to involve the citizens of Reus
A part from the screenings, Memorimage will run a series of side events, such as Memorijove, a section of morning screenings to make the festival approachable to a younger audience; the Memorimage Marathon, a continuous showing of all the awarded films; a Creative Documentary Workshop, open to the citizens that apply, and The making of... case studies of notable films that use archive images.
Memorimage Festival, with its intention to engage the citizens of Reus as much as possible, will be held in various locations within the city. The opening and closing ceremonies will take place in the Bartrina Theatre, the screenings in the Sala Santa Llúcia; the workshop of documentary creation in the CIMIR- Centre for the Image Mas Iglesias, and the workshop for university students in the Centre de Lectura de Reus.
Reus, 8 July 2015.
Daniel Jariod graduated as a filmmaker at ESCAC. He attended a master's degree specializing in Documentary in 2000 in Turin, Bratislava and Amsterdam, where he discovered his love for documentaries.
The X Memorimage edition will be held from 3rd to 7th of November at Bartrina Theatre.
