Disco and Atomic War tells the story of a strange kind of information war, where a totalitarian regime stands face to face with the heroes of popular culture and looses. It is a film about a generation who was unknowingly brought to the front line of the Cold War. Their brains were bombed with television waves.
This completely changed our whole lives. Western popular culture had an incomparable role shaping Soviet children's worldviews in those days - in ways that now seem slightly odd. Finnish television was a window to a world of dreams that the authorities could not block in any way.
Q&A with director Jaak Kilmi.
In May, 1927, an exceptional journey began: the young German girl Clärenore Stinnes, accompanied by the Swedish photographer Carl-Axel Söderström and two mechanics, initiated the first ever journey around the globe on board an Adler Standard 6.
At age 26, Clärenore was the most successful female racing pilot of Europe and was urging for adventure.
After travelling two years and one month, and after 25000 miles, in June of 1929 she arrived to Berlin.
Based on the original footage filmed by Söderström throughout the journey, and a carefully crafted fictionalized dramatization, we join miss Stinnes on a fascinating journey.
Original language: German
During the Third Reich regime more than 1200 films where produced in Germany, a couple of hundred being Nazi propaganda.
Almost seventy years after the end of the regime, more than forty films still are locked up and are only projected under special circumstances.
Using extracts and discussions recorded after the projections of those films, the director shows us how controversial these films still are nowadays.
Can Nazi propaganda still be effective when it is presented to viewers susceptible of being manipulated?
Original language: German, french, hebrew and english
