Memorimageis much more than screening films.
Each year, the festival programsactivities about cinema, memory and footage addressed to various groupsinterested in culture, knowledge, research and entertainment. This way they can actively participate in the festival.
Buster Keaton is the director and the star of this great classic that made silent film history.
In 1861, with the breakout of the American Civil War, Johnny tries to enlist in the Confederate Army, yet the army feels that he will be more useful in the rearguard.
To top things off, he is snubbed by Anabelle, who is convinced that he is a coward.
The engineer will have his only chance to prove his bravery when a command from the North hijacks The General and kidnaps Anabelle. And Johnny won’t think twice to hop on another locomotive to get back the two great loves of his life.
This workshop provides the necessary knowledge tolearn how to build and tell these stories through a documentary.
How would you film it?Learn the key elements for the preparation of a documentary.
Angels Villaris Executive Producer of Parallel 40 where she has developed and produced more than 40 documentaries, many of them films from young filmmakers starting out in the film industry.
Price:€ 55 (including VAT) includes access to all festival screenings.
Those interested in participatingshould send an email tomrogers@memorimagefestival.org with the following information:Name and Surname, phone number, age and profession, four lines with your motivation for attending the workshop.
During the spring of 1938, the international brigades set up camp in El Priorat to prepare the Battle of the Ebre. This period of coexistence and solidarity among soldiers and civilians, full of memories, was reconstructed through testimony, photos and archival footage from the time. The often cheerful way in which those involved experienced the prelude of the last battle.
This is a documentary about life in the rearguard.
Original language: Catalan, English
Travelling across Corb Valley, this documentary focuses on the natural, and also human, landscapes from a Catalonia that had, and still has, a different rhythm to the big cities.
Over the past fifty years, the rural areas of Catalonia have overcome a great process of social and economic transformation that was unthinkable back then.
The generations that have lived these changes have gone from not having any drinkable water or no roads to get out of the village, to high speed trains.
From manual work to machines, from culture of effort to problem-solving technologies, from going on a cart to communicating on Twitter.
Q&A with the director VO Confirm attendance
Querida Dª Elena is a documentary based on the letters of the most popular problem page in the history of Spanish radio. Found abandoned in a country house in Cornellà, those letters now form part of the Sant Feliu De Llobregat County Archive. Querida Dª Elena revisits the customs, ideals, fashions and problems of the Spanish society of the 1950s through the private letters that thousands of women sent to a fictitious Elena Francis, who did not always answer them on the radio when the issue at hand overstepped the prevalent moral and ethical limits of the time.
The screening of this film will be one of the cases of study of The making of... which will include a talk by film director Josep Rovira and archive researcher Montserrat Bailac.
From the beginnings to the Spanish Civil War (1897-1939)
From 8th November 2011 to 22th January 2012
Discover the beginnings of film in Reus and the birth of the city’s first films houses. Bear witness to the way the theatres were occupied by the cinema. Learn that in the first three decades of the 20th century double-header film sessions were combined with vaudeville, and that that the adverts were made in Catalan. Look back at the old gems of the silent film and the landmarks of those years in Reus. These are the invitations and goals of this exhibition, which moreover presents a collection of posters and programmes offered by Joan Ramón Mestre.
She is currently the Head of International Promotion at the Filmoteca de Catalunya.
Mireia Ros (Barcelona, 1956) began her career as a director following many years of experience as a film, television and theatre actress.
In 1992, she founded the production company Bailando con Todos, which has taken on most of her projects.
In 2006, she gained entry to a number of film festivals with her El triunfo.
A journalist specialised in culture and particularly in cinema and art, Carolina’s experience has brought her to collaborate with Radio Nacional, TVE Miramar and Cadena 13, though she has spent the majority of her professional career at TV3 (1984-2011).
Working for the news service at TV3, she has covered the art and film sections, as well as Europe’s main festivals.
She has also taken part in the cultural documentary programmes Trossos and Galeria Oberta.
As a contributor to the programme 30 minuts, she produced a report on the milieu behind the scenes of the different film festivals; and she is the director of the documentary film Te de Tàpies.
Josep-Ignasi Sardà (Barcelona, 1954) holds a degree in Arts from the University of Barcelona. As the translator and head of tourism publications within the Department of Commerce, Industry and Tourism (1990-2005), he was in charge of the communications and projects of the Democratic Memorial Programme (2005-2007) and head of programming and publications of Memorial Democràtic (2008-2011).
He is a member of the jury for the Democratic Memorial Award for Memorimage since 2008, he is nowadays coordinator of Promotion, projects and history advisor of Memory and Peace Section.
The son and grandson of filmmakers and the creator of countless short films, in 1983 Raül received the Spanish Ministry of Culture’s New Filmmaker Award for his first feature film Crits sords, as well as the Catalan Government’s Award for Best Catalan-Language Feature Film of the Year. He has also written and directed a number of plays and has given seminars on film language.
He is currently a board member of Cinema Rescat, the Catalan association for film heritage research and recovery.
As a film club devotee, an independent filmmaker, a participant in countless protests in the 70s and a critic, Roc undertook many different tasks in cinemas around Catalonia, the Balearic Islands and Aragon, and eventually became the Secretary of the Film Awards of the Catalan Regional Government. He was also a member of the evaluation committees of the Film Service and Catalan Films at the 1989 Cannes Festival and at the Berlin Festival in 1991, 1992 and 1993.
Roc Villas moreover helped organize several celebrations of the Sitges Film Festival, which he directed 1999 and 2000. Since 2001, he has been serving as the Director of the Film Library of Catalonia.
For the past twenty-three years, she has been working for TVE-Catalunya, in areas including daily news, reports, documentaries, and investigative journalism.
Her documentaries have received honours such as the Barcelona City Award in 2005, Memorimage 2007 and a Special Mention at the Barcelona City Awards in 2002.
She also served as a member of the international jury for INPUT 2004.
Sue is chair of FOCAL International, the Federation of Commercial Audio Visual Archives. She works for the International Federation of Television Archives (FIAT/IFTA) and is a member of the Royal Television Society’s History group. Formerly Head of the BBC’s Broadcast Archives, Sue currently works as an independent media archive consultant.
She has a wide range of archive work experience and is also an experienced programme research in the BBC Current Affairs and Documentaries production departments.
Born in 1947, Tue worked for more than 20 years at the Danish Film Board and directed the European Documentary Network from its foundation in 1996 to 2005. Member of the jury in various festivals and writer of articles for national and international media, Tue has given documentary seminars in more than 30 countries.
His contribution to the promotion of documentaries has led him to obtain numerous awards, such as the EDN Life Achievement Award 2005. Since 2006 he works as a freelance consultant and teacher.
Josep-Ignasi Sardà (Barcelona, 1954) holds a degree in arts from the University of Barcelona. As the translator and head of tourism publications within the Department of Commerce, Industry and Tourism (1990-2005), he was in charge of the communications and projects of the Democratic Memorial Programme (2005-2007).
A member of the jury for the Democratic Memorial Award for Memorimage 2008, he has served as the head of outreach and communications for the Directorate General for Democratic Memory since 2007.
El Último Magnate (the Last Tycoon) is Horacio Echevarrieta, a great businessman from Bilbao and the most famous entrepreneur in Spain during the dictatorship of Primo de Rivera. Associated with his name are countless iconic locations around the country, including Madrid’s famous Gran Vía thoroughfare, the airline company Iberia, the Barcelona Metro and the Ensanche District of Bilbao.
Echevarrieta became an ally to the German Marine, taking part in their secret plans through the most eminent spies of the time, Wilhelm Canaris and Walther Lohmann. Together, they would devise the plot to defy the Versailles Treaty and against all odds, build the best weapon of their time: the prototype for the German and Russian U-Boats of World War II, the E-1 submarine.
Somewhere amid espionage, political intrigues and other international finances, this historical adventure reveals the strategic alliance that in the 1920s would enable Spain and Germany to develop the most sophisticated weapon technology of their time.
Memorimage 2009 will be programming cases of study for the archive proffesionals, documentalists, journalists, and media proffesionals in general, as well as for the university community (specially students of Social Ciencies and Communications).
These sessions offer an apportunity of learning how the active TV and film proffesionals have created their films/archives and what is their aim.
Each session will feature a film screening or fragments of the film, followed by a presentation by the creators of the film and a subsequent round-table discussion with the public.
