The story of Siegfried Meir could perfectly be a movie.
Born German and Jewish, he was sent to the Auschwitz concentration camp when he was only nine years old.
After the death of his parents, he was sent to Mathausen, the Spanish republican camp, where the young Saturnino Navazo would adopt him and give him a new education.
Siegfried decided to dedicate his life to show Saturnino that it was worth saving him. He succeeded in the music industry as a French singer and was the first person to open a macrodiscotheque in Ibiza in the sixties, frequently visited by personalities like Brigitte Bardot o Jean-Paul Belmondo. Siegrfried is currently a painter and sculptor.
“I still can`t explain how I survived that place, where the only way you got out was from a chimney, reduced to smoke” Siegfried Meir.
Original language: Catalan, spanish and french
Many of us have once-in-a-lifetime recordings, but not the technical knowledge and tools to elaborate our own documentary project.
Memorimage Festival aims to offer participants from Reus and nearby areas the opportunity to create and edit their own documentary films based on the family archive.
The workshop focuses in the recovery of the amateur cinematographic images as a source to narrate personal stories that will contribute to the construction of the memory.
The workshop aims to provide the participants with basic notions to elaborate a script and to edit the selected images, so the participant can elaborate his/her own narrative discourse while learning the editing process when making a documentary film. To participate in this workshop the student must bring material previously prepared and selected, an idea or theme and the title for the project.
Participants: Maxim 12 people.
Duration: 8 hours.
Basic requirements:
-To present in advance a document (Word or pdf) with the title and idea or theme for the project (max. 2 lines).
Program:
- Main concepts of the idea/theme, treatment, structure and sequence.
The Loss reflects the exile of an entire generation of intellectuals, scientists and academic scholars who were forced to flee Argentina, due to the political violence of the 60s and 70s.
The film is structured around interviews with a dozen Argentinean exiles who are successfully working in their host countries.
What would today’s Argentina be like, had it not been deprived of that brilliant generation?
